APS-011 — Policy Exceptions

Document ID: APS-011
Title: Policy Exceptions
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Exception Model (CPEXM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Exception Model establishes the principles, structures, governance, lifecycle, and controls for managing approved deviations from Canonical Policy Objects while preserving accountability, traceability, risk awareness, and engineering integrity.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Exception architecture
Exception requests
Risk assessment
Compensating controls
Exception approval
Exception lifecycle
Monitoring
Revocation

This specification applies to every approved deviation from a Canonical Policy Object.

3. Canonical Definition

A Policy Exception is a governed, explicitly approved, time-bounded authorization permitting deviation from one or more Canonical Policy Objects under defined conditions while maintaining accountability and risk control.

An exception modifies applicability.

It SHALL NOT modify the underlying policy.

4. Objectives

The Policy Exception Model SHALL:

Govern policy deviations.
Preserve policy integrity.
Control operational risk.
Enable temporary flexibility.
Preserve auditability.
Support regulatory compliance.
Enable monitoring.
Prevent undocumented bypasses.
5. Exception Principles

Every Policy Exception SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Explicit Authorization

Exceptions SHALL require explicit approval.

Implicit exceptions SHALL NOT exist.

Temporary Validity

Every exception SHALL define an effective period.

Permanent exceptions SHALL be modeled as policy changes rather than exceptions.

Risk Awareness

Every exception SHALL include a documented risk assessment.

Compensating Controls

Where risk increases, compensating controls SHALL be defined.

Traceability

Every exception SHALL remain permanently traceable.

Revocability

Every active exception SHALL be revocable.

6. Canonical Exception Structure

Every Policy Exception SHALL define:

Property	Description
Exception ID	Globally unique identifier
Policy Reference	Affected policy
Exception Type	Classification
Requester	Initiating authority
Approver	Approving authority
Effective Period	Start and end dates
Risk Rating	Risk assessment
Current Status	Lifecycle state
7. Exception Categories

Atlas defines the following canonical exception categories.

Category	Description
Operational	Temporary operational necessity
Technical	Technical limitation
Regulatory	Regulatory conflict
Security	Security accommodation
Emergency	Immediate response
Business	Business continuity
Experimental	Controlled experimentation

Additional categories MAY be defined through governance profiles.

8. Exception Request

Every request SHALL include:

Request identifier
Applicable policy
Justification
Requested duration
Impact assessment
Supporting evidence
Requested compensating controls

Requests SHALL be independently identifiable.

9. Risk Assessment

Every exception SHALL evaluate:

Engineering impact
Operational impact
Security impact
Compliance impact
Financial impact
Reputational impact
Likelihood
Severity

Risk SHALL be explicitly documented.

10. Compensating Controls

Compensating controls MAY include:

Additional approvals
Enhanced monitoring
Manual verification
Independent review
Temporary restrictions
Increased audit frequency
Alternative safeguards

Controls SHALL remain active throughout the exception period.

11. Exception Approval

Approval SHALL define:

Approval authority
Decision timestamp
Approval rationale
Applicable governance policies
Conditions of approval
Review schedule

Approvals SHALL be immutable.

12. Exception Lifecycle

Every Policy Exception SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Requested
      │
Risk Assessed
      │
Reviewed
      │
Approved
      │
Active
      │
 ┌─────┼─────────────┐
 ▼     ▼             ▼
Renewed Revoked Expired
       │
       ▼
     Closed
       │
       ▼
    Archived

Lifecycle history SHALL remain permanently reconstructable.

13. Monitoring

Every active exception SHALL be monitored for:

Compliance with approval conditions
Expiration
Risk changes
Compensating control effectiveness
Continued business justification

Monitoring SHALL generate Canonical Events when significant changes occur.

14. Renewal and Revocation

Renewals SHALL require:

Updated justification
New risk assessment
Governance review
Fresh approval

Revocation MAY occur due to:

Risk increase
Policy update
Control failure
Regulatory change
Governance decision

Renewal SHALL NOT overwrite historical approvals.

15. Governance

Every Policy Exception SHALL define:

Exception owner
Governance authority
Review authority
Approval authority
Audit requirements
Retention policy

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the exception lifecycle.

16. Quality Requirements

Every Policy Exception SHALL satisfy:

Explicit authorization
Complete justification
Risk transparency
Time-bounded validity
Traceability
Auditability
Governed lifecycle
Effective monitoring

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

17. Conformance

A Policy Exception implementation conforms if it:

Supports governed exception requests.
Requires explicit approval.
Records risk assessments.
Implements compensating controls.
Supports monitoring.
Supports renewal and revocation.
Preserves complete exception history.
18. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted risk analysis.
Predictive exception monitoring.
Automated expiration workflows.
Federated exception governance.
Cross-jurisdiction exception coordination.
Dynamic compensating controls.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical exception semantics.

